  "subject": "🙆🏻‍♀️ Axios Finish Line: Shake it off",
  "date": "Mon, 16 Nov 2026 21:29:21 -0500",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🗼 Plus: Eiffel hour | Monday, November 16, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Nov 16, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n Hello, Monday! Smart Brevity™ count: 429 words … 1½ mins. Edited by Natalie Daher and copy edited by Amy Stern. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: 30-second stress reset \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n I've tried power posing , I've done box breathing , I've taken supplements. Nothing quite shakes off the scaries like somatic movement — and if my Instagram algorithm is any indication , I'm not alone, Axios' Natalie Daher writes .\n\n 🫨 Why it matters: Sometimes the best way to calm your mind is to move your body. A simple technique called somatic shaking may help loosen muscle tension, interrupt anxious thoughts and give your brain a reset. All you need is 30 seconds and a little room to move. \n\n Maybe that's a restroom or beside your bed in the morning, rather than a subway platform, but you do you. Here's how to try it, per University of Virginia psychologist Kim Penberthy and other experts interviewed by TIME : \n\n 👋 Start with your hands. Shake them gently for 10–15 seconds, as if you're flicking off water. 🙆 Get your arms involved. Keep your shoulders loose and let your arms shake naturally. Think inflatable tube man outside a car dealership. OK, maybe not that naturally. 🦵 Shake out your legs. Move one at a time, holding onto something for balance if needed. 😮‍💨 Pause and notice. Do your shoulders feel less tense? Has your mind quieted down? Where might you still be holding tension? The big picture: You can't always think your way out of stress, especially when you're overstimulated. Sometimes a little physical movement can help break the cycle.\n\n The best part? You can do it between meetings, after a difficult phone call or whenever you need a quick reset. 💃 The bottom line: Shaking off what ails us is a small reminder not to take ourselves too seriously — and that even our most anxious moments, too, shall pass.\n\n P.S. Tonight is my last Finish Line! A heartfelt thank-you to Mike, Erica, Amy, our dedicated readers and Axios for letting me share these stories with you over the years.\n\n A few parting words: Marathon-running was a wild quarter-life crisis , chores with friends are way more fun, and it's never too early to start documenting your family history (chosen or biological). Plus: Handling conflict is a skill , and nothing beats an in-person hang . Thanks for reading along. ❤️ \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM AXIOS\r\n\n \r\n Break through the noise with Axios \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Reach execs and business leaders with Axios.\n\n\r\n We'll help you tell your story in the right way:\n\n\r\n \r\n We'll distill your brand's message into its most effective form with Smart Brevity. \r\n No clutter, no filler — just clean, smart and effective. \r\n \r\n Contact us to learn more. \n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 💐 Parting shot: From Paris, with love \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Photo: Madison Mills/Axios 🇫🇷 Friend of the newsletter and Axios senior AI reporter Mady Mills sent us these photos from her honeymoon in Paris earlier this fall. \n\n She captured a spectacular sunset over the Eiffel Tower from her hotel room window and a sunny afternoon at the Jardin des Plantes. Photo: Madison Mills/Axios \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Au revoir! Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
